WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
UI lag and dropped frames are primary symptoms of the legacy react native renderer.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.560
Under heavy load, fluid interactions often stutter or freeze entirely.

3
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.360
View data lives across four environments.

4
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:19.680
JavaScript, C++ yoga, Java, Objective-C, passing across the bridge when changed.

5
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.960
Layout information is serialized into JSON to cross, adding massive overhead.

6
00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.120
With data duplicated four times,

7
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.960
the system requires strict locks to prevent conflict and updates.

8
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.600
These locks create a dependency where the UI and JavaScript threads frequently block one

9
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.400
another, waiting for the other to release layout data.

10
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.760
Cross language serialization created a structural bottleneck that prevented react native

11
00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:50.000
from supporting a concurrent rendering model. React Native 0.76 addresses this with the fabric

12
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.520
renderer. The primary goal is the complete removal of the JSON bridge. Fabric eliminates

13
00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:01.200
the data copy problem by moving the entire view description into a centralized C++ environment.

14
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:07.760
This architecture allows for direct communication between JavaScript and C++ via JSI.

15
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:13.360
The JS thread can now access the C++ core without the delays of a serialization queue.

16
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.480
While native Java and Objective-C views still exist to show the UI,

17
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.120
they have become thin facades that represent the underlying C++ data model.

18
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:29.040
By bypassing the bridge, fabric ensures view mutations are atomic and layouts

19
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:35.360
execute without thread hopping delays. To manage the UI and C++, fabric uses a structure

20
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:40.320
called the shadow tree, which functions as the C++ equivalent of React's virtual DOM.

21
00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.560
Every JSX component you write, like a view or text tag,

22
00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:51.360
corresponds to a specific C++ shadow node. Each shadow node contains props stored in a typed C++

23
00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:57.280
struct. These are generated by Cogen based on your TypeScript specifications. Because these fields

24
00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:02.720
are typed, the renderer no longer needs to perform slow runtime dictionary lookups to find component

25
00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:09.120
properties. The shadow node also contains an embedded yoga layout node. This yoga node is responsible

26
00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.800
for all the Flexbox math, calculating the precise physical metrics like the size and position of the

27
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:21.200
component. Consolidating typed props and layout logic into one C++ struct provides a

28
00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:27.440
direct authoritative source of truth for the UI. Fabric operates on one unbreakable rule.

29
00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.880
Once a shadow node is committed to the tree, it is strictly immutable. To change the UI,

30
00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.600
the system follows a clone on right process. It duplicates the existing node,

31
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:43.360
applies the changes to the clone, and then builds a new tree. This allows for reconciliation,

32
00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:49.200
managing multiple UI trees simultaneously. The current tree is the layout the user sees right now.

33
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.240
The work in progress tree is assembled in the background. The rendering tree is currently transferring

34
00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.920
to native facades. Maintaining these distinct immutable states is the mechanical requirement for

35
00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:06.640
handling complex multi-stage UI updates safely. The immutable shadow tree enables the concurrent features

36
00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:13.440
found in React 18, specifically the ability to perform interruptible renders. Imagine a scenario where

37
00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:19.200
a low priority task, like rendering a massive list of search results, begins building a complex tree

38
00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.400
in the background. If the user taps a button while that search is still calculating, the system needs

39
00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.760
to react immediately. A legacy renderer would finish the layout before processing the tap,

40
00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:36.240
causing unresponsive frames. Fabric instead discards the work in progress tree to prioritize interaction.

41
00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:41.680
It immediately begins building a small new tree for the top event. This is only possible because of

42
00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:47.680
immutability. Since the work in progress tree hadn't been committed, discarding it has no side effects.

43
00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:52.400
The original current tree remains active on the screen, keeping the UI stable while the background

44
00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:59.520
task is aborted. Achieving concurrency requires an interruptible C++ engine that can prioritize human

45
00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:05.760
interaction over background calculations. Fabric executes work across a three-lane threading model,

46
00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.880
the JavaScript thread, the background commit thread, and the UI thread. When state changes,

47
00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:16.320
the update drops from the JS lane into the background via JSI. The background commit thread

48
00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:22.080
calculates yoga layouts for asynchronous events like scrolling. Once ready, it is sent to the UI thread

49
00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:27.840
as a mounting transaction, an atomic set of instructions to update native views. This structure

50
00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.960
allows React to automatically route background data updates to the asynchronous lane while keeping the

51
00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:38.240
main thread clear. This division of labor prevents the deadlocks common in the bridge architecture,

52
00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:43.200
ensuring that heavy calculations do not stall the interface. For the highest priority discrete

53
00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:49.200
events, like a user press, fabric can be even faster. In these cases, fabric bypasses the background

54
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:56.560
routing entirely. The update is processed entirely on the UI thread. This is synchronous rendering.

55
00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:02.640
The state update, the layout calculation, and the native commit all happen in a single uninterrupted

56
00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:07.920
sequence. This allows the screen to update in the same frame the touch was registered,

57
00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:13.280
creating a feeling of instant responsiveness. For developers writing native components,

58
00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:18.720
this speed requires caution. You must never call UI view methods from the background shadow node

59
00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:25.120
threads. This transition from the bridge to a direct JSI pipeline removes the legacy communication

60
00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:31.680
delays that once limited the framework. Fabric moves React native beyond its origins as a view wrapper,

61
00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:39.920
providing a concurrent C++ rendering engine that achieves immediate UI responsiveness.

